\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A few approaches, from simplest to most involved:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 1. Contextual embeddings at index time.
\f0\b0 \strokec2  Instead of embedding the raw subchunk text, embed a contextualized version: prepend a brief summary or the parent chunk's heading/topic sentence to the subchunk text before generating the embedding. Store both the original subchunk text (for citation binding) and the contextualized text (for embedding). This way the vector search already "knows" the context without needing to fetch the parent at query time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 # At indexing time:\
contextual_text = f"\{parent_chunk_heading\}: \{subchunk_text\}"\
embedding = embed(contextual_text)\
\
doc = \{\
    "text": subchunk_text,           # original, for citation binding\
    "contextual_text": contextual_text,  # for understanding\
    "embedding": embedding,           # derived from contextual_text\
    "parent_chunk_id": parent_id\
\}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 This eliminates the parent lookup at query time entirely. The embedding carries the context, and the LLM prompt only needs the subchunk text for quoting.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 2. LLM-generated subchunk summaries at index time.
\f0\b0 \strokec2  Take it further \'97 when you create subchunks, use an LLM to produce a self-contained restatement of each subchunk that incorporates the necessary parent context. Store it as a 
\f2\fs26 \strokec2 self_contained_text
\f0\fs24 \strokec2  field. Use it for embedding and for the LLM prompt context, but still use the original 
\f2\fs26 \strokec2 text
\f0\fs24 \strokec2  field for citation binding. This is a one-time cost at indexing and completely removes the ambiguity problem from the analysis pipeline.\

\f1\b \strokec2 3. Flatten the prompt structure.
\f0\b0 \strokec2  Instead of giving the LLM four separate text blocks (statute subchunk, statute parent, policy subchunk, policy parent) and asking it to mentally juggle scope, concatenate them into two clearly delineated blocks:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 STATUTE REQUIREMENT:\
[parent context, truncated to the relevant paragraph]\
>>> [subchunk text] <\
\
POLICY TEXT:\
[parent context, truncated to the relevant paragraph]\
>>> [subchunk text] <\
\
The text between >>> and <<< is the specific clause under analysis.\
Your policy_quote MUST come from the policy text between >>> and <<<.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 This is a prompt-level fix \'97 visually anchors the LLM to the subchunk while keeping context inline rather than in a separate section it has to cross-reference.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 4. Pre-filter parent chunks into the subchunk record.
\f0\b0 \strokec2  At index time, extract just the immediately surrounding sentences (1-2 before, 1-2 after the subchunk within the parent) and store them as a 
\f2\fs26 \strokec2 local_context
\f0\fs24 \strokec2  field on the subchunk document. At query time you read one document instead of joining to the parent. Keeps context tight and relevant without the full parent chunk's noise.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 The biggest bang for the buck is option 1 \'97 contextual embeddings. It solves both the retrieval quality problem (embeddings are more meaningful) and the runtime complexity problem (no parent lookups) with a one-time indexing cost. Option 2 is stronger but requires an LLM call per subchunk at index time, which may be worth it given this is a compliance-critical pipeline.\
}